{{\textless}li{\textgreater}Christie} et al (2007) The Nutrient Cycle: Closing the Loop, Green Alliance {www.green-alliance.org.uk/uploadedFiles/Publications/reports/TheNutrientCycle.pdf} {[Live} June 2007]{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{\textless}/ul{\textgreater}
{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{\textless}/ul{\textgreater}
{{\textless}h3{\textgreater}Livestock} reduction{\textless}/h3{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}This} is perhaps where the report is most clearly in a Hayekian 'planner mode'. Goal: zero carbon. Solution cannot include ruminant animals. Therefore they must be reduced drastically. {("Despite} historical preferences, Britainʼs citizens are unlikely to spend their entire carbon allowances on beef, mutton and cheese."){\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}But:} hmm - what is the unit of analysis? Yes - you can get aggregate {GHG} emissions, and say where they should be distributed. But that doesn't make it happen.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}But} if a farm is given control over its own carbon? That is, if they can make an overall saving, they can sell credits. E.g. making {GHG} equivalent savings elsewhere; ruminants are putting into ground, rather than taking more {GHGs} for feed.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Three} points there -{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{\textless}ol{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}Carbon} costs can be centrally imposed but{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}It} needs to be done in a way that allows people with knowledge to implement systems that make the savings. i.e. at the farm level.{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{\textless}li{\textgreater}but there also needs to be some consideration of 'structural adjustments' that the individuals have no interest or ability to make, e.g. trading locally in inputs.{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{\textless}/ol{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}The} choice of what to produce is then not centrally taken at all. The alternative is the economic cliche: rather than interest groups competing for money, they'll compete for carbon concessions. This has already happened, of course - it would have been considerably better just to tax carbon, surely?{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}So} there's also a larger question here about what 'big picture' analyses should be aiming to achieve, or can hope to achieve. We desperately need them - but there's plenty of historical precedent for thinking carefully about how we can do them better, reducing political risks and possible unintended consequences / hijacking by special interests. cf rather grim 'tactical sequencing' World Bank example.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Again} [74] - {"Under} the assumed organic shift, there will be a rapid decline of industrial pig and poultry farming in favour of extensive local systems." Conflating expectation and prediction? How much should we pre-judge what a local diet will look like? Demand will still have an impact. See also Krugman on potatoes! http://web.mit.edu/krugman/www/mushy.html - a more readable copy in notes for this.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}h3{\textgreater}Dietary} changes -{\textless}/h3{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}They} predict "a considerable reorientation of the typical British diet."{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Carbon} and fuel costs - "would make processed foods more expensive relative to raw ingredients."{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
