{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Following} Boserup 1965 - {"As} a particular food production system reaches its limits, the affected population can migrate, stay and stablise; stay and decline; or adopt a more productive subsistence. The last option may not initially be any more appealing or probable than the others. When it comes, the shift requires higher energy inputs, so even with higher food production density the net return ratio may decline, but the higher edible energy flux will support a larger population." [148]{\textless}br /{\textgreater}{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}Increase} in energy requirements for each stage of intensification. {\textless}br /{\textgreater}{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}Ploughing:} next to impossible for humans; manual threshing and milling very hard - to supply cities, water and wind power used{\textless}br /{\textgreater}{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}"The} dominance of cereals is thus an energetic as well as a nutritional imperative: they combine fairly high yields, good nutritional value (high in filling carbohydrates, moderately rich in proteins), relatively high energy density at maturity (roughly five times that of tubers), and low moisture content suitable for long-term storage." [155]{\textless}br /{\textgreater}{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}Set-aside} for seed from cereals - often very high for medieval crops, e.g. 1/3 to 1/2.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Harvesting:} the most time-consuming / labour intensive task where still hand-harvested. E.g. South China - 94-98\% of all labour engaged between March and September. In other places too - {"This} need could be met only if all members of a farming family worked arduously long hours or by relying on migratory labour. These peak labour demands were among the most important energetic bottlenecks of traditional farming." [155]{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Still} the case: story of Scottish crops rotting in ground due to lack of labour - tis an interesting point. {\textless}br /{\textgreater}{\textless}br /{\textgreater}p.156/8 for various details on horse powers, jobs, hectare rates. Very useful! Get this into the model.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Great} advance in various horse-drawn ploughs and harvesters - up to the logistical limit of 16 to 40 horses. Key point - notes that the 'useful work' done when humans did it is always the same - harvesting e.g. "about 1.3 t / ha of wheat"; efficiency of 15-20\% for both humans and beasts.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Early} C19th: 1h labour aided by {9MJ} of draft work; by the end of C19th in e.g. California, 1h of labour controlled {220MJ} of horsework. key quote:{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}"Farmers} ceases to be the key energizers of the process and became controllers of larger energy flows." [161]{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{\textless}p{\textgreater}...{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
