{{\textless}p{\textgreater}"In} no case do the average power production densities of renewable conversions surpass {100W/m2."} [311]{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}The} maximum seems to be high-course hydro at 5-15w/m2 and solar heat collectors in sunny locations, the same.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}"In} contrast, extraction of fossil fuels produces coals, crude oils, and natural gases with power densities of 1 - 10 {kW/m2.} Modern civilisation is thus energised largely by fuels extracted with power densities 1-4 {OM} higher than can be deliverd by renewable flows." [311-2]{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Most} of the renewables produce electricity not fuel - portability is different. {(My} word.){\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}"Most} coal-fired power stations operate with densities of 1-3 {kW/m2,} but proper assessments should include the entire cycle of fuel, transportation, generation and transmission." [312] He mentions: on-site coal storage (can see this at Drax); "decades of fly ash deposits" (165ha in his example); voltage and length of transmission lines.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Narrow} seams vs opencast - entirely different densities; "the rights of way of a dedicated railroad may claim much more land than the plant and coal extraction, as would a new long transmission line." [312]{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Notes} that, taking all this into account, some may start to border on solar plants in hot regions - "but the comparison shifts in favour of any thermal station once the intermittence and stochasticity of renewable flows is taken into account." {(With} hydro is the only real exception, remaining pretty constant.){\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Nuclear:} basic plant = {300MW/m2.} Plus cooling towers and on-site waste storage, 4kw/m2. But - "the need for substantial low-population zones around the plants clearly limits the type of land uses in their immediate vicinity while not physically claiming any of the affected land. Moreover it remains uncertain what areas will ultimately be claimed by long-term depositories of radioactive wastes." [313] So again hard to measure.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}"All} of these uncertainties, and even more so the considerable ranges of actual power densities for every major category of harnessing and converting commercial energies, mean that any proffered global total of the land claimed by the energy conversion and distribution can only be a very rough quantitative estimate hiding many qualitative differences." [313]{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Smil's} attempt at a calculation: 2000 - 300,000km2, Italy-size, 0.25\% of the Earth's ice-free land. 10\%  = actual extraction and processing of fuels; more than half = reservoirs. Many have multiple uses including irrigation. [so a kind of energy multiplier effect?]{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}"Leaving} the reservoirs and transmission rights of way aside, the global fossil-fueled system with throughput of about {11.5TW} and overall land claims of no more than 75,000km2 would average about {150W/m2.} {[I} think he's only talking about electricity generation...]{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}So} no longer {OMs} above renewables...?{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}In} {US,} a better estimate: closer to {200W/m2.} He also notes - {HV} power lines take up as much land as pipelines.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Some} consumption power densities:{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{\textless}ul{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}10MW/m2} - large power plant boilers and blast furnaces "a large continuous fuel supply is paramount for the operation of these giant fuel convertors that provide key economic inputs." [314]{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}20-100W/m2} - domestic housing {[US} presumably] and low-density / low energy industry / offices etc.{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}High} rises = higher power density (this helps us how? A very geog idea, but...){\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}Typical} urban power densities: 10w/m2 in uncrowded, low-income cities to 100w/m2 in affluent crowded areas.{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{\textless}/ul{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Section} on water may be useful too - e.g. total global food production needs 6000km3 of water, 2/5 coming from irrigation. [321]{\textless}/p{\textgreater}}
