@misc{wagenhofer_we_2005,
	title = {We Feed the World},
	author = {Erwin Wagenhofer},
	year = {2005},
	annote = {{{\textless}p{\textgreater}We} Feed the World notes{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Austria.} since joining {EU} 1/4 of farmers quit.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}We} see wheat being harvested, then an absolutely huge amount of bread being thrown out - a mountain a day.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Much} talk of "fair price for our product" - complaint about consumer's expectations about cheap food.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{\textless}p{\textgreater}2 million kilos of decent bread throw away every year.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{\textless}p{\textgreater}4/5 wheat for Austria imported from India, according to {US} special rapportuer for food rights{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{\textless}p{\textgreater}10\% fallow requirement imposed by {EU:} fallow land subsidised by {EU.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}}
{\textless}p{\textgreater}"this fish is, as they say in the trade, only for selling, not for eating."{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{\textless}p{\textgreater}"why tomatoes travel 3000 kilometres across Europe, why Africans migrate to the wealthy northern countries."{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Almeria,} S Spain - capital of winter vegetables. Began in the 60s: Madrid laid on the water supply, told peasants to come down from the mountains and work. {"The} government supplied the water and the agronomists."{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Greenhouses:} 25,000 hectares, more than Belgium and Holland together.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Oh} my God - the greenhouses from the Air are incredible - I must find that image.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Substrate} of rockwool, plant 4-5 months old. Tomatoes I think. Roots penetrate through the rockwool; nutrients and water come through on a dripfeed. Water is recycled: disinfected, then fed back through.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{\textless}p{\textgreater}9 or 10 metre high tomatoes plants. Cripes.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}"We} know exactly what goes into the dripfeed. If we planted it in the soil, then we wouldn't know exactly. There are more diseases in the soil. The substrate is better."{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}"It} started here because there's so much sunshine - over 3000 hours here in Almeria. In Brussels, by comparison, there are only 1000."{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}It} used to be a poor region - now villages expanded from 1000 to 100,000 people. "no-one's a millionaire, but they can make a living."{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Jean} Ziegler, {UN} special rapporteur on the right to food: {EU} and {US} subsidise food production and export - 349 billion dollars a year. Dumping - in places "where there's almost nothing but peasant agriculture." Gives the example of Dakar in Senegal. City has largest agricultural market in West Africa, Sandagar Market. You can buy European vegetables there for a third of the local prices.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}"So} the Senegalese peasant, even if he works himself into the ground for 18 hours a day, under the burning sun, hasn't got a chance of being able to survive by working his own land. So what can he do? If he's still got the energy, he risks his life as an illegal immigrant via the Straits of Gibraltar and has to hire himself out somewhere or other in southern Spain or work as a streetsweeper in Paris in unhumane conditions."{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}We} see an immigrant spraying greenhouse crops liberally, hanging the sprayer over his shoulder - no protective clothing here.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Workers} living in a shantytown fashioned out of disused greenhouses. They burn plastic crates to keep warm; conditions are appalling.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Romania:} for farmers, horses are still used for everything. We talk to Kark Otrok, Director of Production at Pioneer's Romania branch, "the largest seed producer in the world."{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}He} notes the clash: horsedrawn carts and their own aircon {4WD.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}}
{\textless}p{\textgreater}"and the peasants can only afford a horse-drawn cart and an ordinary plough; there's no money for anything else."{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}We} see hand-scything. Wow. {"It's} like going back in time, say about 50 years."{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Hand-harvesting} of aubergines; though note even industrial scale, something like this would have to happen...{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}He} compares a hybrid aubergine - looks very good, good for display. But he reckons the taste is nothing compared to ones grown from saved seed every year. Er... what kind of seed company does he work for?{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}"If} you compare the seed costs, this one only costs the effort needed to extract the seeds and dry them. The seeds costs 15 euros for ten grammes. But it looks wonderful. And when people shop they're taken in by its appearance. That's only natural. It looks much better in a shop display. There's no way of telling which is better, you can only buy and taste yourself. For me, the local one's better."{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}"I} hope that this won't change too fast in the near future. I think it will change, though, because of the many large foreign corporations establishing themselves and introducing hybrids. They're in fact destroying what's been built up here, everything that's natural. For example, the aubergines, sweet peppers, or tomatoes which are still natural and have nothing to do with these hybrids. It's the way they work, too, that fascinates me. The people, all these people who still work the land, and harvest and sow by hand. The standard of food will suffer - so will the flavour. It'll no longer be as it used to be and should be. Children will have no memory of what a proper tomato tastes like, or an apple, or anything. Everything will taste different. You can't stop progress, and we don't want to. That's how things are. It just goes on and on and on until one day it'll all come to an end."{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Wow} - this guy is so amazingly conflicted!{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Seeds} from hybrids cannot be used for a second sowing.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}He} waxes lyrical about the impact on food large corporations are having: destroying natural systems and seed stocks; destroying a memory of what decent flavour is. Cripes.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Romania:} 2nd largest agricultural country after France; 3.5 million ha producing seed for maize. Potential for 75\% expansion.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}"Last} year hybrid seeds were subsidised by the state. This year there were no subsidies, the farmers have to pay the full price. That means that the state makes its own farmers dependent on this product because the seed from the hybrid forms can't be used to sow another crop."{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Classic} pusher trick.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{\textless}p{\textgreater}60 employees, 50 cents an hour on this small farm. Onions there: offered to Dutch at 27 cents; they can get them for 17 cents - because of subsidies.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}"What} we call beo..." meaning back to artisanal production?{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}I} imagine he's not in post any more - keeps on encouraging farmers to reject the hybrids!{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Here} he is: "we fucked up the West, and now he we coming to Romania, and we'll fuck up all the agriculture here." Followed by {"I'm} 100\% behind what the management of the company wants, but here I'm voicing my own private opinion. That's the way I see it." Not so private any more!{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}He} then shows us a 60 hectare field of Roundup Ready {GM} Soya. So everything dies except the Soya. This has to be metaphorical. Er. cf. Scott, of course.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Soya:} mainly used for animal feed, various processed foods. Already used in chocolate.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}"We} let them die so we can live."{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Pioneer} in 120 countries, now in China. 2nd largest after Monsanto.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Pioneer} slogan: {"We} feed the world."{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}"A} company is a company and a company doesn't have a heart."{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Now} in Brazil, following trucks full of Soya. Driven thousands of miles to be exported to {EU,} China, Japan.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}World's} largest Soya producer is in Mato Grosso Brazil: the Maggi Group. The owner? The governer of the state of Mato Grosso.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Jungle} cleared solely to grow Soya.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}"Our} soil is good, but it's no good for soya. We have to bring in all the nutrients and apply them artificially.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Plane} flies over vast areas of forest cleared, ground prepared for planting soya.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Soya} producers demanding govt build an asphalt road through one of the last original areas of jungle. The money is being provided by the World Bank and the {Inter-American} Bank.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}"European} livestock is eating up the rainforest of Amazonia and Mato Grosso."{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Jean} Ziegler again: {"You} don't see the day-to-day fear for the next day. In Brazil there's a custom, among the mothers in Northeastern Brazil in the shanty towns of the poverty-stricken states. When the children are crying with hunger in the evening, the mothers put a pan of water on the fire, put some stones in it and boil these stones. then the mothers say: 'wait, wait, supper will be ready soon,' in the hope that their starving children will go to sleep, and stop crying in the meantime. That happens every day, repeated a thousand times."{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Same} old story: everyone getting sick from drinking water near heavily treated crops.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}We} see children who are being kept from starvation by milk from one goat.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Brazil:} home of the Green Revolution, yes? People starving.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Chicken} broiler parents: now only 3 companies worldwide that sell them.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Industrial} production of life with the eggs - all vast machines and transports.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Broiler} chicken man: sees increasing regionalisation of food production towards large-scale comparative advantage.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}He} sees competition from South America and Asia - best cuts can be sold internationally, but they can "dispose of the cuts that are not so popular to the domestic population."{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Ziegler} again: "free trade has nothing to do with freedom, that's an enormous lie. It's the freedom of the predatory animal in the jungle when Nestle, for example, takes on an African farmers' syndicate. That's like Mike Tyson going into the ring against an undernourished Bengali. And the corporations, the power of the corporations in today's world, is expressed in a figure published last year by the World Bank. Last year, 52\% of the gross world product, was controlled by 500 global corporations."{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Hey}  - People and Planet were making that point years ago. I believe it even got acted out on several occasions.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Peter} Brabeck, head of Nestle: is he still? Anyway -{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}"Today} people believe that everything that comes from Nature is good. That represents a huge change because until recently we always learned that nature could be pitiless. Man is now in the position of being able to provide some balance to Nature, but in spite of this we have something approaching a shibboleth that everything that comes from Nature is good. A good example is the organic movement. Organic is now best. But organic is not best. After 15 years of eating {GM} food products in the {USA,} not one single case of illness has occurred from eating them to date. And in spite of this we're all so uneasy about it in Europe, that something might happen to us. It's hypocrisy more than anything else."{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Gosh.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}}}
},

@article{bassi_integrated_????,
	title = {An integrated approach to energy prospects for North America and the rest of the world},
	volume = {In Press, Corrected Proof},
	issn = {0140-9883},
	url = {http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/B6V7G-4W4TY0R-2/2/29091546ab295957c7cef3df89a49f14},
	doi = {10.1016/j.eneco.2009.04.005},
	abstract = {Many international organizations and research institutions have released recently unequivocal scenarios on energy's future prospects. The peak in global oil production is likely to happen in the next ten to fifteen years, if it hasn't already happened, and decisions to be made in the near future are likely to have large impacts on our quality of life in the coming decades. This study presents an integrated tool for national energy planning customized to North America. The authors analyzed the impact of world oil production on economic, social and environmental indicators. Two cases of global ultimate recoverable oil reserves are considered, a low and medium estimate within current research. Three sets of policy directions were chosen: Business As Usual {(Market} Based), Maximum Push for Renewables, and Low Carbon Emissions. Results of the simulations show that without restrictions on emissions coal becomes the dominant energy in the longer term. On the other hand, if {US} policymakers are able to effectively implement the necessary polices, such as a 20\% {RPS} by 2020 and increased {CAFE} Standards, along with increased energy conservation and efficiency, the medium to longer-term economic impacts of a global peak in oil production can be mitigated, while a sustained reduction in emissions would require a larger effort.},
	journal = {Energy Economics},
	author = {Andrea M. Bassi and Robert Powers and William Schoenberg},
	keywords = {energy}
},

@article{leonard_integrating_2008,
	title = {Integrating Sustainability Practices Using the Viable System Model},
	volume = {25},
	issn = {1092-7026},
	url = {http://apps.isiknowledge.com/full_record.do?product=WOS&search_mode=Refine&qid=3&SID=Q14FabgiPAbeHddNO5j&page=4&doc=35},
	doi = {10.1002/sres.937},
	abstract = {This paper represents an effort to explore the use of Stafford Beer's Viable System Model {(VSM)} to design human communities that foster adaptation to criteria of sustainability in our natural and social environments. With the projected rise in sea level and other changes that accompany warming temperatures, it seems probable that many communities, even some large cities, will have to be abandoned and their populations relocated. This difficult circumstance could create tens of millions of climate change refugees. It could be met with the failure and incompetence that characterized and still characterizes the response to Hurricane Katrina or it could provide an opportunity to rebuild in a manner that combines a low impact on the environment without sacrificing quality of life. Using the framework of the {VSM,} three levels of recursion will be explored: the household, the neighbourhood and the city. It will be possible to draw upon lessons learned about building and maintaining cities in different climates and under different conditions over the centuries and from the construction of 'new towns' in the past 50 years. It has been characteristic of communities that regularly endured environmental challenges to have fostered means of collaboration and co-operation to address them and to constrain competition within bounds that did not threaten their common survival. There is no shortage of ideas and designs that could be applied but there is not yet either the political or social infrastructure to integrate and implement them. Steps taken in this direction might help to coalesce the necessary political will. The following are proposed as guidelines for design using the {VSM.} The community and the city's external relationships with the surrounding natural, social and economic environments should be such that they can support themselves while avoiding endangering their own survival and well being or that of their surrounding environments. Their internal operations should be such that an adequate quality of life is available to everyone from the most dependent members of its society to the most productive. They should pursue these ends with a view to maintaining a balance between collaboration/symbiosis and competition and with the fewest restrictions on the autonomy of members consistent with the other guidelines. The {VSM} can be both a template for design and a framework for discussion of what sustainable communities in a time of climate change might look like. Such discussions would be facilitated by Beer's Team Syntegrity process that he invented as a companion to the {VSM.} The process provides a whole system, high variety structure that gives equivalent status to each participant and viewpoint. This high level of communication will help to provide the cohesiveness that is needed when choices must be made and hardship shared in order to achieve a new equilibrium with the environment. Copyright (c) 2009 John Wiley \& Sons, Ltd.},
	number = {5},
	journal = {{SYSTEMS} {RESEARCH} {AND} {BEHAVIORAL} {SCIENCE}},
	author = {A Leonard},
	month = oct,
	year = {2008},
	keywords = {jacobs},
	pages = {643--654}
},

