@misc{ellerman_jane_????,
	title = {Jane Jacobs as a Development Thinker},
	url = {http://creativeclass.typepad.com/thecreativityexchange/files/david_ellerman_jane_jacobs_on_development.pdf},
	author = {David Ellerman},
	keywords = {economics, localisation},
	howpublished = {http://creativeclass.typepad.com/thecreativityexchange/files/david\_ellerman\_jane\_jacobs\_on\_development.pdf},
	annote = {{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Pulling} out agglomeration bits{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{\textless}ul{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}Notes} that Glaeser [1992] supports Jacobs ext. but that it goes way beyond 'urban economics.' [2]{\textless}br /{\textgreater}
{\textless}ul{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}"Cities} and their regions, rather than nation states, are the main sites of development."{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{\textless}/ul{\textgreater}
{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}He} cites Jacobs talking about Napizaro in Mexico sending people to work in Los Angeles {[Jacobs} 1984 p.76] Many people have considered starting their own companies based on the skills they acquired but -{\textless}br /{\textgreater}
{\textless}ul{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}"The} skills and experience the men have acquired in Los Angeles are usable only in the context of a city economy with its symbiotic nests of suppliers and its markets, not in an economically barren region." {\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}Of} course, there *could* be a situation where there was a local market to be exploited. But note the point: connection of skill to work; context it's in; elasticity of labour substitution; all self-reinforcing in a Krugman-like way [3]{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{\textless}/ul{\textgreater}
{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{\textless}/ul{\textgreater}
{\textless}p{\textgreater} {\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{\textless}ul{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}Development:} not just 'growth' but 'differentiation, diversification, and transformation in the products and in the underlying processes of production - all of which might be hidden in the black box of 'total factor productivity'. {\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}"Aggregate} growth theory in economics does a disservice to the understanding of development by abstracting away from the difference between growth and development." [5]
{\textless}ul{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}Another} implication of this I keep on returning to at the moment: you can have development whilst shrinking. The negative growth dynamic just triggers different factor flows and thus different avenues for development.{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{\textless}/ul{\textgreater}
{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}Preformation} (growth) epigenesis (development){\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}Note} how all this connects to agglomeration and spreading - how does it happen in actual geographies?{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}Development:} "more like the process of epigenetic transformation, not blowing up a small ballon - with more K and L - to make a big balloon." {\textless}br /{\textgreater}
{\textless}ul{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}Compare} with the claim that the core model is Jacobian: nooo, not really. It's just not {MARian.{\textless}/li{\textgreater}}
{\textless}/ul{\textgreater}
{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{\textless}/ul{\textgreater}
{\textless}p{\textgreater} {\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{\textless}ul{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}Rainforest/desert} - same amount of sun; desert just re-radiates heat back out at night. {\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}Ecological} analogy: imports analogous to incoming energy. {"Lacking} the free energy of the sun, there must be some exports of commodities or services to fund the imports." [6]
{\textless}ul{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}[Might} want to think more about the explicit rel to energy]{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{\textless}/ul{\textgreater}
{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}Settlements} that are "rather straightforward economic conduits" [6] - q: could I measure this via transport intensity? Possibly - and it connects space directly to this notion of development.
{\textless}ul{\textgreater}
{\textless}li{\textgreater}cf {"A} settlement is more like a 'rainforest' when the imports feed into a diversified web of local value chains  some goods being inputs into many other products or spawning import-replacements. The imports spread out like a river delta to directly and indirectly feed a diverse weblike 'ecology' of economic activities."{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}Compares} to tangled bank; not sure of Jacobs herself ever uses the phrase, but I'm using it!{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{\textless}/ul{\textgreater}
{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}“To} become more ramified and complex, an economic settlement should have multiple uses for imports to produce diversified and multi-staged products with a significant part for local use. Each specialization to achieve efficiency should lead to diversification of outputs into various product niches, to backward integration to produce previously imported inputs, and perhaps to unexpected 'matings' with nearby processes and products to produce novel offspring.4 These are the innovations from "human capital externalities" that tend to happen when diverse people with various skills and complementary knowledge jostle together in companies, and companies jostle together in cities.” [7]
{\textless}ul{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}Re.} human capital externalities - how does analytics get at this, if it does at all? Cf. Baumol book, the free market innovation machine. {\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}Happens} internal to some firms - Jacobs' example of {3M;} {"This} process in which one sort of work leads to another must have happened millions of times in the whole history of human development." {[Jacobs} 1969, 53] [8]{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{\textless}li{\textgreater}"an example of the dynamics of agglom that goes beyond increases in L and K and that goes on inside the expanding black box of 'total factor productivity.' "{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{\textless}/ul{\textgreater}
{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{\textless}/ul{\textgreater}
{\textless}p{\textgreater} {\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{\textless}ul{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}Connecting} division of labour and new discoveries - "the process of deepening the division of labour in the old work leads to a new type of work, a new branch on the tree."{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}Jacobs} e.g. wire bristles to card wool leading to pin-making; pin-making subsumed by machine in the end [schumpeter.]{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}So} - "the story of the {\textless}em{\textgreater}dynamics{\textless}/em{\textgreater} of development is not the static efficiency of greater specialisation but the branching off of new kinds of work."{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{\textless}/ul{\textgreater}
