{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Christina's} parents: oxen for pulling, and a cow - most vital.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}C.13th:} markets starting to open, currency becoming commonplace - peasants could sell surplus.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}St.} Albans market: was there in 1086.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Christina's} parents rented a market stall.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{\textless}p{\textgreater}1267: King allows villagers to have a market. Fish and everything! Fleshmongers. "growth of capitalism at the grassroots."{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Beginning} of England tax system - also, pop went from 1086 - 1286: {2M} to {6M.} Christ!{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Tax} system: organised on 100s, division of the shire. Don't get it. Codicote: 29 taxable people in {1307.Hugh} Cok: Christina's father - taxable. 13 pence and 3 farthings. Doubled in 30 years.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Christina's} parents: also brewed and sold ale. Traditionally a woman's job. Quite a careful process. Their main, daily drink. Many things were added: nettles, dandelions... You were a brewster.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Controlled} by the ale-tasters.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Ralph} the miller, and Christina, sold ale contrary to what they said: were fined. All very bureaucratic...{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}A} lot of very local litigation: some form of money raising.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Christina's} landlord. Church: huge landowner. Separate church courts, called consistory court. St. Alban's abbey - ancient wealthy centre of power and privilege. Site of the oldest Christian site in the {UK?{\textless}/p{\textgreater}}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Monks:} endowed by saxon king Offa in C8th; lands of codicote - given to Monks in 1002 by Ethelred the unready. Codicote - owned by king and then church.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}The} abbot: gosh, powerful monk!{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}A} peasant needed to know the law, if to have an advantage.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{\textless}p{\textgreater}14th century: peak of pressure on land - population has really grown.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{\textless}p{\textgreater}1314: slip into catastrophic climate change - horrible winters, rain - "great famine." 2 centuries of cooling, Europe's little ice-age. Through july, august, september - 1315-16. Paid in ale to get workers to extend harvest time.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Charity:} lacking - lord e.g. docking beer allowances; cutting food rations to workers because of price of corn.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{\textless}p{\textgreater}1316: worst for the people - best for the landlord's profits.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Would} have seen "flooded valleys, flatten fields, ruined crops. And in places, the food distribution system simply broke down. Merchants from as far away as Yorkshire were travelling through the home counties desperately trying to buy up the last precious supplies of grain."{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Farmers} would have been tenants: how to pay the landlord and feed the family? Livestock would have to be sold because they couldn't be fed - so a buyer's market, low prices.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Landlord's} repossessions in codicote: up from 6 to 38 in 1316.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{\textless}p{\textgreater}10\% of the pop died - 1/2 M.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}How} to insure against starvation? Edible plants around house - edible weeds, bristly ox-tongue. Not nice, but insurance. If you had a property, you might just hold things together this way.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}The} miller - did alright even in hard times. Rhyme:{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}John} the miller grinds small small,{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}The} king of heaven sees all, all{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Pay} a tithe: c.10\% of grain - miller prosperous, prob universally hated as a result.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}"What's} the baldest thing in the world? A miller's shirt, for every day it clasps a thief by the throat."{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Miller:} tax raiser for the landlord. How to circumvent?{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Hand-quern:} "frequent cases in the court-books of Christina's neighbours being done for using them."{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Grind} small quantities for domestic use. If the abbot caught you, you could have your grain confiscated, your horse confiscated - or the stone itself.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}"On} one famous occasion the abbot sent his bailiff and his men to the villages around, including Codicote, to confiscate quernstones and take them back to St. Alban's, where he used them to pave his new patio."{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}The} abbot sent people to re-value the village and people after the famine. Christina in her 40s with teenage children. She doesn't have any of the lands and strips that her father had. Just 1p rent. 1p in order to make a fence. Etc.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}She} sets up her daughter with a house and a shop.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{\textless}p{\textgreater}25th June 1348: black death - arrived in Weymouth, all the way from India. Moved a km a day. Nearly half of the {UK} died. Whole villages disappeared from the map - but with less people, peasants had bargaining power.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}They} stormed the Abbey, demanding an end to serfdom. They wanted to destroy the records, of their subjection: their leader - William Grindcob: "all we want is a little liberty, after so many centuries of oppression."{\textless}/p{\textgreater}}
},

@misc{strader_simulation_1998,
	type = {{Text.Article}},
	title = {Simulation of Order Fulfillment in Divergent Assembly Supply Chains},
	copyright = {{JASSS@soc.surrey.ac.uk}},
	url = {http://jasss.soc.surrey.ac.uk/1/2/5.html},
	author = {Troy J. Strader and {Fu-Ren} Lin and Michael J. Shaw},
	year = {1998},
	note = {Management of supply chains is a difficult task involving coordination and decision-making across organizational boundaries.  Computational modeling using multi-agent simulation is a tool that can provide decision support for supply chain managers.  We identify the components of a supply chain model and implement it in the Swarm multi-agent simulation platform.  The model is used to study the impact of information sharing on order fulfillment in divergent assembly supply chains (commonly associated with the computer and electronics industries).  We find that efficient information sharing enables inventory costs to be reduced while maintaining acceptable order fulfillment cycle times.  This is true because information, which provides the basis for enhanced coordination and reduced uncertainty, can substitute for inventory.},
	howpublished = {http://jasss.soc.surrey.ac.uk/1/2/5.html}
},

