@misc{_file_2008,
	title = {File on Four: are food prices the result of speculation?},
	month = may,
	year = {2008},
	annote = {{{\textless}p{\textgreater}File} on Four, Tuesday 20 May {20:00{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}==={\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Ghana:} four big flour mills. Producers of wheat in {US} and Canada - that\&apos;s where the prices are coming {from.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}High} wheat prices in Ghana. Since Feb / March - 40\% of production down in one firm because no-one can afford to buy the {bread.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Takaradi} Mills: Armenian-owned. He knows price affected by drought etc. But now he thinks its not due to this - there\&apos;s something else going {on.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Speculation} blamed: drought = \&apos;spark that started a fire\&apos;. {{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Prices:} Chicago mercantile exchange is where prices are set, {NOT} {Ghana.\&nbsp;\&nbsp;The} exchange appears to still be people shouting \&apos;buy!\&apos; {\&apos;sell!\&apos;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Price} boards around the walls. Buying / selling futures contracts. Hands away = selling; hands towards = {buying.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Commodity} futures. To buy at a specified date at a set {price.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&quot;A} wall of new money flooding into commodities\&quot;. A huge amount of cash going in because price is going {up.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Money} sloshing around: Chinese growth, Arab oil? Commodity index funds channel the money. They own about 20\% of the {US} corn {market.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&quot;Fundamentals} were important to start the bull {market.\&quot;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Now} in central Illinois, where wheat is {grown:{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Corn} farmer, American corn-growers\&apos; association. Gary Neema: uneasy about the price, though he stands to benefit. Price of corn gone up from 4 to 6 dollars / bushel in five months. Every day the funds buy {more.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&quot;Fundamentals\&quot;} not there: no shortage of supply, apparently. 10\% is now in store. Production has been increasing [but in response to market {signals?]{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Two-year} old corn: wasn\&apos;t sold. No shortage of corn. \&quot;about the half the corn left we took in this {fall.\&quot;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}But} the price still goes up. {\&quot;More} buyers than sellers in spite of the price. The market\&apos;s honest: someone\&apos;s willing to pay that {price.\&quot;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}That\&apos;s} the speculators. {{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Mr} Duffy from the Chicago Market: he say no! Major crop failures around the world. It cannot be blamed on the index fund, he {says.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Having} no speculators = \&quot;communism at its best!\&quot; {Er...{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Now} we\&apos;ll over-produce, so prices will go down after they\&apos;ve gone {up.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Hearings} on Capitol Hill: {{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Back} on Ghana: petrol prices n public transport. Petrol price has doubled in one year. Half salary on transport alone. Eek. {{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Petrol} prices means even the cheapest food - casava - goes up because of transport costs.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}1 gallon petrol = 1\% of Ghanian income. Little Ghana can do about oil {prices.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Hard} to argue that petrol\&apos;s gone from \$65 to \$130 and say that\&apos;s due to {fundamentals.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Another} argument: {OPEC} may not be telling the truth about that quantity. They stick to the same prediction about their reserves year to {year.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&quot;There} is no oil, there is no rice. Food inventories are the lowest they\&apos;ve been for 60 {years.\&quot;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Hedge} Funds: little known market, growing exponentially. \$7.1 trillion on the books of banks - \&apos;over the counter commodity derivative.\&apos; 70 times the size of the {US} strategic oil {reserve.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}No} controls over \&quot;over the counter\&quot; derivative markets as they are with banks. {{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&quot;Oil} is easily the biggest of all the traded commodities.\&quot; Crude futures. {{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Similar} to the sub-prime market. Apparently. Contracts can be sold, sub-divided, etc. {\&quot;A} mirage of things you can do.\&quot; Does it matter? Yes: there\&apos;s an unknown risk.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}}
},

@article{coe_global_2008,
