@book{morgan_worlds_2006,
	title = {Worlds of Food: Place, Power, and Provenance in the Food Chain},
	isbn = {0199271585},
	shorttitle = {Worlds of Food},
	publisher = {Oxford University Press},
	author = {Kevin Morgan and Terry Marsden and Jonathan Murdoch},
	month = jan,
	year = {2006},
	keywords = {food},
	annote = {{{\textless}p{\textgreater}California:} first, new agrarian {capitalism{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&quot;From} the 1850s, California became the first and most complete example of industrialised agriculture, which promoted an agri-industrial complex based on a hierarchical and diverse division of labour, from the farm to the factory. This created an \&apos;integrated business system\&apos; which involved the flow of materials through commodity chains...\&quot; {113{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Good} quote from Stoll 1998 [the fruits of natural advantage: making the industrial countryside] {p.181{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&quot;California} fruit business represented industrial farming at its apex: the almost complete separation between farm production and consumption and the dedication of soils in a vast region to consumers far away. Though nature presented a set of ecological options making possible a great diversity, the growers\&apos; particular reading of nature led them to plant a limited number of plants in monocultural strands. Determined to enjoin California with the emerging national economy, they invested in labour practices, chemical inputs and market-organisations intended to sustain specialised crops. People and nature served the growers in a singular capacity, but the growers refused to serve either in return.\&quot; {[110]{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Early} development: chief obstacle of half a million indigenous reduced to 10,000 \&apos;within a century\&apos;, \&apos;free\&apos; labour (imported from Mexico), competitive markets. {\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}1925: 136,000 farms, many urban in origin. {\&quot;Strong} links to urban finance houses.\&quot; Profits, money, flows to centres - San Francisco and Log Angeles. {\&quot;In} short, an agri-industrial complex was born.\&quot; \&apos;rapid centralisation of capital\&apos;.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}3 key features outlined by Marx, a \&apos;pure\&apos; form of capitalist development: \&apos;private property controlled by a capitalist class, the exploitation of wage labour, and monetary investment for profit.\&apos; [113]{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}},
	annote = {{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Notes} from Tuscan {chapter{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}===={\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}A} note that Tuscany might be an example of an \&apos;interpersonal world\&apos; suffering under attack from {EU} hygiene legislation. {{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Here\&apos;s} an attempt to get enough details to aid a typography. Think I\&apos;ll photocopy it {too.\&nbsp;\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Sharecropping} (mezzadria, going as far back as 13th / 14th Century) is, it is claimed, responsible for the particular entrenched use of the {countryside:{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&quot;According} to Pratt (1994) the mezzadria comprised an urban landlord who provided the farm, the farmhouse, and half the working capital, and a rural tenant who, along with his family, provided the other half of the working capital and the labour. While the sharecropper had some autonomy in terms of the labour process, the landlord regulated the flow of investment and oversaw the processing and marketing of products. All the production from the farm was divided equally between landlord and tenant.\&quot; {[91]{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Landlords} owned large estates containing many holdings, and managed them all from a central fattoria. This is a \&quot;system that permitted regular investment in the farms by the landlord, a factor that was particularly important for the cultivation of wine and olives, where there is some considerable timelag between initial cultivation and subsequent {output.\&quot;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Landlords} were often urban and their share went into urban areas; the mezzadria was more focused on subsistence. This production was highly varied so families could survive all year round. A quote from Pratt again {[Pratt,} the rationality of rural life] -{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&quot;most farms kept cattle, sheep, pigs, rabbits, chickens, pigeons. A patch of woodland provided household fuel for cooking and grazing in high summer. Arable land produced wheat, barley, oats, maize, beans, hay, and a variety of other crops to give oxen their working strength. Vineyards and olives were created by terracing the steeper slopes, while figs, walnuts, almonds and a dozen kinds of fruit tree were common. A kitchen garden supplied onions, garlic, carrots, and all the other vegetables and herbs needed for a diet centred on bread and vegetables.\&quot; [92 Pratt p {39]{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Thus} profoundly shaping the countryside. Note post {WWII} political struggle, underpinned by Communist {Party.\&nbsp;\&nbsp;Pratt:} \&quot;the mezzadri first wanted to wrestle from the landlords greater control over estate management then a greater share of the product, and finally ownership itself.\&quot;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}1940s: Christian Democrat govt does push for land reform - aim: from subsistence to production. Reform agency: built roads, leased equipment, including tractors. Quickly succeeded in increasing Tuscan {productivity.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}So} - 1950s: abolition of mezzadria, land reform, leads to restructuring of Tuscan farms. Sharecroppers become owner-occupiers. Shift to wage, rather than sharecropping, {labour.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&quot;Moreover,} as farmers became better integrated into urban markets, more money became available for further investment, so that by 1961 almost one-third of farms owned their own tractors.\&quot; {[93]{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}EC} and onwards: more rationalising, moving away from sharecropping to employees, new investments in {infrastructure.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Would} like some evidence for this: \&apos;thus, it was believed that only by encouraging a rural exodus and a further integration of existing holdings could agriculture be rendered more economically efficient.\&apos; [93] Really? Twould be good to read some stuff on this. Farms did remain small - averaging 29 hectares, too small for mechanisation. Pratt: \&apos;they were also too small for the labour available, and this led to a gradual population decline and the search for off-farm employment.\&apos; New economic activities in the cities pulled people in {too.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Question:} so... the land could support people before at subsistence, but production became more efficient, needing less labour? Thus people drawn to {cities?{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Pratt:} \&apos;the poverty and isolation of rural life compared to an increasingly known urban alternative led to a rural {exodus.\&apos;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Agri-tourism} now occurs on a grand scale: what does this do to the spread of trust and information in Tuscan {products?{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Great} quote on Brunello di Montalcino wine by a large producer. They were attracted to the area following Italian govt designation of the product / region as {\&apos;Denominazione} {d\&apos;Origine} Controllata\&apos; - here\&apos;s an estate manager at Villa Banfi:{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&quot;precision was a key goal... precision in the spacing of the vines, in the height of the fruiting spurs, in the volume of the fertilisers applied and in the timing of the harvest. It was achieved through the best available laboratory which provided a flow of data on the chemical needs of the vines, on the best shoots to select for grafting and on the ripening process of the grapes. Variability was a source of irritation, whether it was found in the unpredictability of manual labour or found in nature.\&quot; [101]{\textless}/p{\textgreater}},
	annote = {{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Tuscany\&apos;s} relation to the state{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}1. Post {WWII:} top-down attempt from state to standardise along modernising lines.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}2. Bottom-up still there.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}3. 1972 - new tier of local government , regional control of agriculture very important. {{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Things} already happening: {\&quot;Picchi} 2002 [in Living Country Sides] notes that local authorities, chambers of commerce, social movements and other such groupings were already working cooperatively within regional committees prior to the 1972 legislation. These committees served to link together policy proposals and the likely impacts on specific territories across Tuscany. As a result, \&apos;a solid coherence between political programmes and projects and the territories involved could be constructed... the territory functioned as the \&quot;matrix\&quot; within which policy was to be discussed.\&apos; {(Picchi} p.206) \&quot; {[104]{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Interesting:} very institutionally managed landscape. {{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}End} of the 70s: intermunicipal associations were formed. Focused on \&quot;supposedly homogeneous geographical areas so that policy proposals could be precisely tailored to differing spatial circumstances.\&quot; Picchi reckons there was lots of co-operation involved. Short-lived, though - only lasted a decade before powers transferred to {provinces.[p.104-5]{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}End} of 1990s - Rural Development Regulation. \&quot;... the whole process was driven by extensive local involvement across stakeholder groups.\&quot; {[105]{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}The} {\&apos;Tuscan} model\&apos;:{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&quot;a territorial, rather than sectoral, approach... a holistic, rather than segmented, view of the relations among economic, social, cultural and environmental factors. it is based on \&apos;subsidiarity\&apos;... and recognises that subsidiarity is especially important in the agri-food context because food production necessarily entails use of the land.\&quot; {[Er,} so does the production of everything else... is modern food any more localised?]{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}},
	annote = {{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Wales,} {UK{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}==={\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&quot;To} a greater extent than in other European countries, the supermarkets have become the key players in shaping food consumption patterns in the {UK.} As in California, retailer power is now the key to understanding the enormous asymmetries of power that punctuate the British agri-food chain from farm to fork.\&quot; {[143]{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&quot;One} reason why supermarkets seem to wield so much more power in the {UK} than their analogies in other countries is that there is less countervailing power at the production end of the {UK} food chain. In contrast to other European countries, where farmers seem to have forged durable and robust cooperative structures, British farmers seem to have been unable or unwilling to sacrifice their individual identities for more collective power as producers.\&quot; {[143]{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&quot;For} many primary producers in the {UK} the \&apos;market\&apos; means not the anonymous and well-balanced forces of a neoclassical mechanism in equilibrium, but a profoundly imbalanced supply chain in which a small number of retailers exercise ever more control over what is produced, where, and by whom.\&quot; {[148]{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Cites} {KPMG} study - growing share of dairy products sold through small number of retailers = imbalance of power in market \&quot;with the result that a smaller proportion of the retail price is passed back to dairy farmers. Such enormous power generally increases the speed at which farm gate prices follow the market down and, conversely, slows the speed at which they follow the market up.\&quot; The House of Commons seemed to conclude the same thing... prices only go back up \&apos;following direct action by farmers\&apos;. [milk pricing in the united {kingdom]{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}That\&apos;s} really interesting! And maybe there\&apos;s data, too. Reactivity of prices within supply chain - exactly my {bag.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Retailer\&apos;s} margins have increased significantly as against producers. Er... chart? Milk development council 2004, Dairy Supply Chain Margins, who made {what?{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Talks} about \&apos;commodity bias\&apos; vs \&apos;high value specialist\&apos; -\&nbsp;\&nbsp;but how many producers could do the latter? Seems a little woolly to me...{\textless}/p{\textgreater}}
},

