{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Counter:} they do. The existence of e.g. walrasian auctioneer doesn't change the fact people were attempting exactly what Hayek says they weren't - a bottom up analysis. Yes, it's a specific case. I'm unclear what Hayek is saying is the problem. Policy prescription from that? Possibly. Though mainly I think choosing what the right launchpad should be for defending the free market (the catallaxy, not perfectly competitive markets producing pareto-optimal equilibria.){\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Questions} they ask: "how could Hayek's insights and theories help to understand {ACE} research? And what, if anything, could we learn from current {ACE} research about Hayek's work?"{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{\textless}p{\textgreater}4: 'central fact' of economics is distributed interactions; analytics unable to touch this with a bargepole, seems to be the gist.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{\textless}p{\textgreater}5: "the question of the modelling of the perceived opportunity sets of the individual agents becomes of central concern."{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Notes} that, without perfect knowledge, some inductive reasoning / learning / adapting would appear to be necessary.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{\textless}p{\textgreater}"... rather than analyzing whether an equilibrium exists for an economy with some {\textless}em{\textgreater}given{\textless}/em{\textgreater} structure, in {ACE} one analyses how structures and patterns {\textless}em{\textgreater}emerge{\textless}/em{\textgreater} as regularities in the process of interaction of the individual agents." {[Err} - same for economics in some senses; just different things are given, different things endogenised.]{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{\textless}p{\textgreater}6: quotes Hayek, who seems to be describing an algedonic response - no change of plans without changes in what's presented to agents. See Use of Knowledge// "as long as things continue as before..."{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}h3{\textgreater}Their} model{\textless}/h3{\textgreater}
{\textless}p{\textgreater}is interesting. Explicitly trying to get to Hayek's points - or some of them. Making some good points about collecting information and acting on it [p.7], c.f Jacobs. Drawing a direct line between Hayek quotes and model points; a little bit simplistic? Wouldn't it be better to do rather more analysis of Hayek's overall viewpoint before doing this?{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}They} equate limited sampling of information with Hayek's case for bounded rationality / 'imperfection of man's knowledge' [8]{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Cunning} modelling trick:{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
