{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Vriend} links Hayek {[LLL} vol 1 p.18 - I've got that!] to Holland et al [86]. Hayek picking up on the evolutionary nature of successful practices.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Note} a point about Hayek from that: evolution can get hold of any number of kinds of structure; the individual level is hardly a primary one. Rules, institutions etc. Not sure how I get this into the {PhD.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}[Remember} I'm looking for some simple {ABM} choice to highlight a comparison with analytic methods.]{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Vriend} links 'bounded rationalilty' to Hayek's 'antirationalistic approach' where people's errors are "corrected only in the course of a social process." [1948b] cf "constructing such rules by deduction from explicit premises." [11]{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Holland} et al 1986: "induction" comes up a lot. Must be good!{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}[See} my notes from week 3 / 18th Jan in field diary: this zero-sum between deductive reasoning in a universal knowledge world and inductive reasoning, I'm not sure about.]{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Notes:} though each rule is simple "it is the {\textless}em{\textgreater}set {\textless}/em{\textgreater}of rules that forms the link between actions and previous actions and outcomes, and it is not the individual rules that matter." [11]{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Picks} out a difference between Hayek and {CS} / Holland approaches: Hayek talks about group selection. {[Does} he solely mean that?]{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Good} question (based on experiments that the model comes from): {\textless}strong{\textgreater}how do people change rules after they experience outcomes?{\textless}/strong{\textgreater}{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Graphs:} the "specific rules as best replies" graph is genius. Takes account of which rule on each tick {\textless}strong{\textgreater}would have{\textless}/strong{\textgreater} produced the best outcome, and compares to what agents chose. The genius: the agents couldn't have known this information, but the modeller can. Rules that copy others improve over time as, overall, rule choice improves.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
