@misc{everett_bes_????,
	title = {The {BES} Agricultural Ecology Specialist Group e-list: Zero Carbon Britain and the implications for agriculture},
	url = {https://www.jiscmail.ac.uk/cgi-bin/webadmin?A0=BES-AGRI-ECOL-GROUP},
	author = {Sue Everett and Graham Russell and Bill Grayson},
	keywords = {agroecology, climate change},
	annote = {{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Full} email discussion as of 21st April 09{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{\textless}p{\textgreater}==={\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{\textless}div {class="PlainText"{\textgreater}{\textless}span{\textgreater}Bill,} it is frustrating that although there is a lot of  good working going on in the area of future farming systems there is not yet a  feeling of a collaborative effort involving scientists, practitioners and policy  makers. There are also contributions to be made from agricultural historians so  we can learn from the past. Small communities should be able to feed themselves  {(I} imagine that in the future some island communities currently looked upon as  poor and remote will become areas of prosperity due to the availability of  renewable energy and investment in capital projects to develop sustainable and  productive agriculture). The big challenge is what to do with the cities, and in  particular how to close the nutrient cycle. The comments in the previous post  were, as you probably realised, about the apprent change in definition of  organic farming from an emphasis on closing cycles to one of not applying  particular chemicals. I find it bizarre that I can be offered "organic" produce  from China, for example. The emphasis on {GHG} emissions can easily hide an  underlying weakness relating to other inputs such as phosphorus and water.  Energy balances for agriculture are tricky to interpret because the status quo  represents some sort of economic balance and does not represent the lower energy  inputs that might be used if costs and prices (and legislation/rules) were  different.{\textless}br /{\textgreater}{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}In} Europe we are lucky that transport by water is an option  and although it is less used than it might be, there are some signs of change.  Here on the river Forth the port of Grangemouth is being transformed into a hub  for supermarkets with foodstuffs being brought in by sea and transferred to  rail. This is nothing new. In Elizabethan times grain was being imported to the  {UK} from the Black Sea area and now we have the {Danube-Rhine} canal. In the 18th  century, apples were exported from Tayside to London by sea but these orchards  have now all disappeared. So some products can be traded at a low energy cost.  With regard to closing nutrient cycles, I know that in the 19th century there  were dairy units in the City of Edinburgh (and no doubt other cities) with grass  and other feed being brought in and manure being taken back (the coming of the  railways and canals was important for this). {\textless}br /{\textgreater}{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}All} these issues could be  explored in case studies which could establish what types of agriculture would  be appropriate in which area. {\textless}br /{\textgreater}{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}Best} wishes,{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}Graham{\textless}br} /{\textgreater}{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}Dr} {G.Russell}  {FRMetS} {MIEEM{\textless}br} {/{\textgreater}School} of {GeoSciences{\textless}br} {/{\textgreater}Centre} for Environmental Change and  Sustainability{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}The} Crew Building{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}The} University of  Edinburgh{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}Edinburgh{\textless}br} {/{\textgreater}EH9} {3JN{\textless}br} /{\textgreater}0131 651 7164{\textless}br /{\textgreater} {\textless}br /{\textgreater}{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}-----Original}  Message-----{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}From:} The {BES} Agricultural Ecology Specialist Group [{\textless}a {href="redir.aspx?C=0f8a0acfb8f04997942e23de007e78af\&amp;URL=mailto\%3aBES-AGRI-ECOL-GROUP\%40JISCMAIL.AC.UK"{\textgreater}mailto:BES-AGRI-ECOL-GROUP@JISCMAIL.AC.UK{\textless}/a{\textgreater}]}  On Behalf Of Bill Grayson{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}Sent:} 20 April 2009 22:06{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}To:}  {BES-AGRI-ECOL-GROUP@JISCMAIL.AC.UK{\textless}br} {/{\textgreater}Subject:} Re: Zero carbon Britain - farming  {[Scanned]{\textless}br} /{\textgreater}{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}Several} points worth serious debate here Graham and as a  practising organic farmer of many years standing I would be glad to take them up  at some stage.{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}More} immediately though I think your  suggestion to try out  some options on a few case studies is really useful. This is something that  all  of the transition town initiatives should be aiming to undertake as part of  devising the Energy Descent Plans that constitute the basis of their overall  strategy for carbon reduction. I know of at least one that has made an attempt  at devising a strategy for feeding itself {(Brampton,} Cumbria) but their may well  be others too. Some strategic collation of their findings or co-ordination of  their efforts could yield some very worthwhile insights{\textless}br /{\textgreater}{\textless}br /{\textgreater}best  wishes{\textless}br /{\textgreater}{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}Bill{\textless}br} /{\textgreater}{\textless}br /{\textgreater}{\textless}br /{\textgreater}{\textless}br /{\textgreater}----- Original Message -----{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}From:} {"Graham}  Russell" {\&lt;g.russell@ED.AC.UK\&gt;{\textless}br} {/{\textgreater}To:}  {\&lt;BES-AGRI-ECOL-GROUP@JISCMAIL.AC.UK\&gt;{\textless}br} {/{\textgreater}Sent:} Monday, April 20, 2009 5:09  {PM{\textless}br} {/{\textgreater}Subject:} Re: Zero carbon Britain - farming {[Scanned]{\textless}br} /{\textgreater}{\textless}br /{\textgreater}{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}Sue,} I  have finally got round to reading chapter 13. Here are some fairly random  thoughts which I hope will encourage further discussion.{\textless}br /{\textgreater}{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}I} was surprised  that there was no mention of Land Capability for Agriculture as all crop types  cannot be grown everywhere. The 60-70\% of land for grazing includes a large  proportion of land that is unsuitable for anything else (including forestry) and  which may already be managed for game and conservation. Areas of permanent grass  are usually that because of limitations that make them unsuitable for arable  cultivation - it is unclear where the extra hectares of arable land will come  from. Short rotation coppice will be treated like a crop and will presumably be  harvested mechanically so it is a competitor for the arable land. Taking short  rotation forest and woodland together means an increase of this from 2 to 6 m  ha. The woodland area in the {UK} is currently {(September} 2008) 2.8 m ha, most of  which is not on farmland - expansion would have to be on the area currently  under permanent pasture. Expansion in the uplands is complicated by the need to  protect peat areas. Livestock play an important role in traditional farming as  the grass leys they depend on help retain soil organic matter and protect the  soil structure, which is particularly important in the wetter parts of the {UK.It}  would be nice to think there could again be intensive fruit and vegetable  production near the cities.{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}Unfortunately} much of the land that was ideal for  this purpose was also ideal for building roads, houses and airports.{\textless}br /{\textgreater}{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}It}  would be interesting to take a set of 10-20 areas, each of say 25 km{\textasciicircum}2 randomly  chosen from across the {UK} and compare the current land use with what would be  possible for a range of scenarios.{\textless}br /{\textgreater}{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}I} am worried that organic and low  input systems are seen as the only answer.{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}Organic} systems, and there are  actually few true organic systems in the original sense of the concept (closed  cycles), require very high management skill if they are to achieve the stated  goals. Levels of emissions per tonne of produce can be higher than other systems  and low input systems can make inefficient use of scarce resources. It is a  complicated issue to which there are no simple answers. Farming systems of the  future will no doubt include elements taken from organic farming but may well  use some inputs disallowed for organic systems. High yielding crops return more  organic matter to the soil in the form of roots and stubble than low yielding  ones.{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}In} cooler parts of the {UK,} nitrate fertiliser permits the earlier onset  of growth of grass and crops as the temperature threshold for growth is rather  lower than that for N mineralisation. What works best in one agro-ecological  situation may not be best elsewhere.{\textless}br /{\textgreater}{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}It} is unclear what climate change  will mean for agriculture itself as the main factors affecting yield and quality  are not simple measures of temperature and rainfall but more complex derived  variables such as weather at harvest and conditions affecting the carry-over of  pests and diseases over winter.{\textless}br /{\textgreater}{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}If} I remember correctly, in the days of  the Norfolk four course rotation when horse power was essential, the livestock  were included to recycle fertility and the only profitable crop sold off the  farm was wheat, one year in four (or fewer in some areas). About a third of the  arable area was needed to provide oats to feed the horses. I seem to remember a  colleague and I both working out that a similar proportion of land would be  needed to run tractors on bio-diesel.{\textless}br /{\textgreater}{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}As} the chapter points out, the  'ghost hectares' are very important. However, it is not inevitably true that  these lead to an increased carbon footprint as the big energy transport costs  come when the crop product starts being moved by lorry, wherever it comes  from.{\textless}br /{\textgreater}{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}Finally,} a lot depends on whether a free market system or a planned  economy is envisaged as it is the financial situation that largely drives what  crops are grown.{\textless}br /{\textgreater}{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}This} is a really important topic that needs to be  underpinned by good science and evidence.{\textless}br /{\textgreater}{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}Best}  wishes,{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}Graham{\textless}br} /{\textgreater}{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}Dr} {G.Russell} {FRMetS} {MIEEM{\textless}br} {/{\textgreater}School} of  {GeoSciences{\textless}br} {/{\textgreater}Centre} for Environmental Change and Sustainability The Crew  Building The University of Edinburgh Edinburgh{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}EH9} {3JN{\textless}br} /{\textgreater}0131 651  7164{\textless}br /{\textgreater}{\textless}br /{\textgreater}{\textless}br /{\textgreater}{\textless}br /{\textgreater}--{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}The} University of Edinburgh is a charitable body,  registered in Scotland, with registration number  {SC005336.{\textless}br} /{\textgreater}{\textless}br /{\textgreater}{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}-----Original} Message-----{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}From:} The {BES} Agricultural  Ecology Specialist Group [{\textless}a {href="redir.aspx?C=0f8a0acfb8f04997942e23de007e78af\&amp;URL=mailto\%3aBES-AGRI-ECOL-GROUP\%40JISCMAIL.AC.UK"{\textgreater}mailto:BES-AGRI-ECOL-GROUP@JISCMAIL.AC.UK{\textless}/a{\textgreater}]}  On Behalf Of Barbara Smith{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}Sent:} 12 March 2009 10:01{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}To:}  {BES-AGRI-ECOL-GROUP@JISCMAIL.AC.UK{\textless}br} {/{\textgreater}Subject:} {FW:} Zero carbon Britain - farming  {[Scanned]{\textless}br} /{\textgreater}{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}-----Original} Message-----{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}From:} Sue Everett [{\textless}a {href="redir.aspx?C=0f8a0acfb8f04997942e23de007e78af\&amp;URL=mailto\%3aconservation.news\%40googlemail.com"{\textgreater}mailto:conservation.news@googlemail.com{\textless}/a{\textgreater}]{\textless}br} {/{\textgreater}Sent:}  11 March 2009 21:24{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}To:} {agricultural@BritishEcologicalSociety.org{\textless}br} {/{\textgreater}Subject:}  Zero carbon Britain - farming {[Scanned]{\textless}br} /{\textgreater}{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}Members} of the ae group may be  interested in the following:{\textless}br /{\textgreater}{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}Zero} Carbon Britain - an Alternative Energy  Strategy was recently published.{\textless}br /{\textgreater}{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}Chapter} 13 covers agriculture. It  envisages major changes in land use.{\textless}br /{\textgreater}{\textless}br /{\textgreater}{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}There} are 20m hectares of land  devoted to agriculture in Britain, used roughly as follows:{\textless}br /{\textgreater}6m ha arable  (including set-aside and rotational grass) 6m ha permanent grass 6m ha rough  grazing 2m ha woodland 2m ha roads and buildings{\textless}br /{\textgreater}{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}The} strategy envisages  substantial changes, as follows:{\textless}br /{\textgreater}10m ha arable rotation (incl. rotational  grass; no set-aside) 1m ha permanent grass 1m ha short rotation willow coppice  {(SRC)} 3m ha short rotation forest {(SRF),} e.g. ash coppice, conifers 2m ha rough  grazing 3m ha woodland 2m ha roads and buildings{\textless}br /{\textgreater}{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}The} full report can be  downloaded from {\textless}a {href="redir.aspx?C=0f8a0acfb8f04997942e23de007e78af\&amp;URL=http\%3a\%2f\%2fwww.zerocarbonbritain.com"} target="\_blank"{\textgreater}www.zerocarbonbritain.com{\textless}/a{\textgreater}.{\textless}br /{\textgreater}{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}The} work is continuing over  the summer and each sector is to be the subject of ongoing research (including  internships) and a seminar. The agriculture seminar will take place in early  September. Following the culmination of all this work, a final report will be  issued prior to the Copenhagen climate summit in December.{\textless}br /{\textgreater}{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}As} an {MSc}  student at {CAT} I have just made contact with the guy leading on this project and  I would be very grateful for feedback from {BES} agro-ecol members and any others  about this report. There is a major issue which isn't really touched upon and  that is the logistics of attaining the envis    aged scenario. On this matter I  would be particularly interested in hearing your views! It may be that the {BES}  a-e group and others can feed into this work and increase its  robustness.{\textless}br /{\textgreater}{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}Regards{\textless}br} /{\textgreater}{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}Sue} Everett{\textless}br /{\textgreater}{\textless}br /{\textgreater}--{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}Sue} Everett{\textless}br /{\textgreater}122  Derwent Road{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}Thatcham{\textless}br} {/{\textgreater}Berkshire} {RG19} {3UP,} {UK{\textless}br} /{\textgreater}{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}Tel:} +44 (0) 1635  857165{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}Mob:} 07779 204015{\textless}br /{\textgreater}{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}Email:}  conservation.news@googlemail.com{\textless}br /{\textgreater}{\textless}br {/{\textgreater}Links:{\textless}br} /{\textgreater}{\textless}a {href="redir.aspx?C=0f8a0acfb8f04997942e23de007e78af\&amp;URL=http\%3a\%2f\%2fwww.meadowmaker.me.uk"} target="\_blank"{\textgreater}www.meadowmaker.me.uk{\textless}/a{\textgreater}{\textless}br /{\textgreater}{\textless}a {href="redir.aspx?C=0f8a0acfb8f04997942e23de007e78af\&amp;URL=http\%3a\%2f\%2fwww.floralocale.org"} target="\_blank"{\textgreater}www.floralocale.org{\textless}/a{\textgreater}{\textless}br /{\textgreater}{\textless}a {href="redir.aspx?C=0f8a0acfb8f04997942e23de007e78af\&amp;URL=http\%3a\%2f\%2fwww.wildmeadows.org.uk"} target="\_blank"{\textgreater}www.wildmeadows.org.uk{\textless}/a{\textgreater}{\textless}br /{\textgreater}{\textless}a {href="redir.aspx?C=0f8a0acfb8f04997942e23de007e78af\&amp;URL=http\%3a\%2f\%2fwww.britishwildlife.com"} target="\_blank"{\textgreater}www.britishwildlife.com{\textless}/a{\textgreater}{\textless}br /{\textgreater}{\textless}/span{\textgreater}{\textless}/div{\textgreater}},
	annote = {{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Notes} on email discussion{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{\textless}p{\textgreater}==={\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}h3{\textgreater}Start:} Sue Everett, Msc student{\textless}/h3{\textgreater}
{\textless}p{\textgreater}... asking for input into {ZCB} ch.13 on agriculture - I think I'd like to set up a meeting to talk this through.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}CAT} has a specific strategy - an end goal. She notes {"{\textless}span{\textgreater}There} is a major issue which isn't really touched upon and  that is the logistics of attaining the envisaged scenario. On this matter I  would be particularly interested in hearing your views!"{\textless}/span{\textgreater}{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}h3{\textgreater}Reply} 1: Graham Russell, geosciences, Edinburgh{\textless}/h3{\textgreater}
{\textless}ul{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}Lack} of {'Land} Capability for Agriculture' - what crops can be grown?{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{\textless}li{\textgreater}60-70\% grazing land, plus grasses - much not suitable for any other crop use{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}So} where will the extra arable land come from?{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}Important} role of livestock in overall agro-system e.g. adding manures etc{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}"Intensive} fruit and veg production near the cities..." - sadly, in competition with roads, houses and airports!{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}Proposes:} take 10 to 20 areas, 25km2 randomly chosen, compare with "what would be possible for a range of scenarios." Note: kind of what I might be doing - very ballpark sort of "what if" based on certain food production systems.{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}Does} not think 'true organic' systems are necessarily the answer - they "require very high management skill if they are to achieve the stated goals."{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}Emissions} levels can actually be higher than other systems. {\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{\textless}li{\textgreater}but "it is a complicated issue to which there are no simple answers."{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}{\textless}span{\textgreater}"Farming} systems of the  future will no doubt include elements taken from organic farming but may well  use some inputs disallowed for organic systems." {\textless}br /{\textgreater}{\textless}/span{\textgreater}{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}{\textless}span{\textgreater}"High} yielding crops return more  organic matter to the soil in the form of roots and stubble than low yielding  ones." [so - another point about how things feed factors into other things...]{\textless}/span{\textgreater}{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}{\textless}span{\textgreater}Nitrates} - may change timing of growth onset (can't quite make sense of the reason why - I think because in warmer climes, N mineralisation occurs naturally, but is affected by temperature.){\textless}/span{\textgreater}{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}{\textless}span{\textgreater}Norfolk} 4 course rotation: horse power essential - livestock "recycled fertility" - only profitable crop = wheat, 1 yr in 4. One third of arable land needed to feed horses. {(What} about cattle?){\textless}/span{\textgreater}{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}{\textless}span{\textgreater}Russell:} worked out similar land needed to feed horses as tractors. {(What,} for same horse-power?){\textless}/span{\textgreater}{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}{\textless}span{\textgreater}"{\textless}/span{\textgreater}{\textless}span{\textgreater}Finally,} a lot depends on whether a free market system or a planned economy is envisaged as it is the financial situation that largely drives what crops are grown." Er...{\textless}/span{\textgreater}{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{\textless}/ul{\textgreater}
{{\textless}h3{\textgreater}Reply} 2: Bill Grayson, organic farmer{\textless}/h3{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Notes} that Transition energy descent plans often include self-feeding goals; Brampton, Cumbria, is quoted.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{{\textless}h3{\textgreater}Reply} 3: Graham Russell again{\textless}/h3{\textgreater}
{\textless}ul{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}"{\textless}span{\textgreater}It} is frustrating that although there is a lot of good working going on in the area of future farming systems there is not yet a feeling of a collaborative effort involving scientists, practitioners and policy makers. There are also contributions to be made from agricultural historians so we can learn from the past." {\textless}/span{\textgreater}{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}{\textless}span{\textgreater}Big} problem: how to close the nutrient cycle?{\textless}/span{\textgreater}{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}{\textless}span{\textgreater}He} talks about change in emphasis of organic farming from closing these cycles to "not applying particular chemicals."{\textless}/span{\textgreater}{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}{\textless}span{\textgreater}"The} emphasis on {GHG} emissions can easily hide an  underlying weakness relating to other inputs such as phosphorus and water." {\textless}/span{\textgreater}{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}{\textless}span{\textgreater}Notes} the difference between energy balance and economic balance.{\textless}/span{\textgreater}{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}{\textless}span{\textgreater}Maybe} more of a future use of waterways in the {UK.} {\textless}br /{\textgreater}{\textless}/span{\textgreater}{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}{\textless}span{\textgreater}Notes} that "some products can be traded at low energy cost." -{\textless}/span{\textgreater} 
{\textless}ul{\textgreater}
{\textless}li{\textgreater}{\textless}span{\textgreater} Here on the river Forth the port of Grangemouth is being transformed into a hub for supermarkets with foodstuffs being brought in by sea and transferred to rail. This is nothing new. In Elizabethan times grain was being imported to the {UK} from the Black Sea area and now we have the {Danube-Rhine} canal. In the 18th century, apples were exported from Tayside to London by sea but these orchards have now all disappeared. So some products can be traded at a low energy cost. {\textless}/span{\textgreater}{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{{\textless}li{\textgreater}{\textless}span{\textgreater}C19th} Edinburgh: city dairy farms - feed bought in, manure taken back: closed nutrient cycle. Pretty high energy though.{\textless}/span{\textgreater}{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{\textless}/ul{\textgreater}
{\textless}/li{\textgreater}
{\textless}/ul{\textgreater}
{{\textless}p{\textgreater}{\textless}span{\textgreater}Connect} these notes to notes from the Zero Carbon Britain report.{\textless}br /{\textgreater}{\textless}/span{\textgreater}{\textless}/p{\textgreater}}
},

