@misc{_bloggingheads.tv:_????,
	title = {Bloggingheads.tv:  Marglin talks community to Will Wilkinson},
	url = {http://bloggingheads.tv/diavlogs/9128},
	keywords = {audiovisual, community, economics, Hayek},
	annote = {{{\textless}p{\textgreater}Notes} and transcription from {webcast{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Marglin:} outlines his barn-raising example -{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}contrast Amish barn-raising to the guarantee insurance gives. The latter = more econ efficient; the former, Marglin argues, is damaged by the latter. The Amish reject all {insurance.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Me:} cf. \&apos;community resilience\&apos; vs resilience through insurance. Both = resilient in different ways. Does the former only apply to the \&apos;in {group?\&apos;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Wilkinson:} goes on to say his whole philosophical learning from Hayek to Douglas North / Vernon Smith = the transition from regimes of personal to impersonal {exchange.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&quot;The} big story of modernity is this transition from small communities that are based on reciprocity, reputation, tight signalling of solidarity through certain kinds of ceremonies and sacrifice to this more abstract, extended order that runs on mechanisms of insurance and credit ratings and things like that, that allow for the extent of economic exchange to expand massively. There are huge gains from the massive expansion of the scope of the market and the intensity of the division of labour. So if they\&apos;re right about the history, a lot of the prosperity we enjoy and a lot of the level of well-being we enjoy is partly a function of making this transition to impersonal {exchange.\&quot;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Marglin} on Hayek (though he writes about it in the book too) - {{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&quot;His} great contribution I think is his understanding of the role of experiential knowledge in his \&apos;the use of knowledge in society\&apos;. This was his response to a debate which is now ancient history really, about the possibility of efficient allocation of resources in a socialist economy. {//{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}I} regard him as the winner in the sense that he had the most important insight. Namely, the argument for efficient allocation in a socialist economy basically said something like this: you don\&apos;t need real markets. You can imitate markets on an \&apos;as if\&apos; basis using paper {calculation.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Hayek} argued that you need markets in order to mobilise the kind of experiental knowledge that economic agents have, in a way they may not be aware of in a way they can put down on paper. This I regard as the great virtue of markets. para: These markets have bought prosperity for the last 400 {years.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}If} I have this great view of Hayek, what\&apos;s my criticism? They\&apos;re manifold. First of all, especially in the rich countries - and I\&apos;m not talking about India or China or Africa or large parts of Latin America. We in the rich countries have relatively little need for further increase in our material standard of living. We have the basis for a decent, dignified standard of life for all our citizens. We don\&apos;t need further {expansion.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Number} 2: I believe there\&apos;s an upside and a downside. The downside of a further expansion in the economy is precisely what I argue in the book - that it undermines community. We\&apos;ve lost the balance and we need to restore that {balance.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}The} argument I\&apos;m making is about community, but it\&apos;s clearly reinforced by the ecological issues we\&apos;re facing - not only climate change, but also resource exhaustion. Now, that\&apos;s a huge problem, and outside the scope of my book. {{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Wilkinson} asks: what the good of the community is? What is it that\&apos;s being lost? What\&apos;s so good about communities that we need to be so concerned {about?{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Marglin\&apos;s} answer: what\&apos;s being lost is human relationships. That\&apos;s it in a {nutshell.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Wilkinson:} asks what data he has to show that human relationships are eroding in a damaging way. Why should we have cause to be {worried?{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Marglin:} 1. what happens to the elderly. Lucky ones: clean nursing homes. Unlucky ones: dirty places, bleurgh. {\&quot;Very} few elderly people can hope to spend their last days connected to their families.\&quot; This is nobody\&apos;s fault, he {adds.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}[How} might that price be measured? How might it relate to food networks\&nbsp;\&nbsp;/family support {networks?]{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Makes} point about not being economically feasible to spend time with children, going back to {work.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Wilkinson:} has worked on how to measure happiness and life satisfaction. He does note the rel betw family structure and economic opportunity. Argument: people getting wealthier - attitude to family changes. From solidaristic production unit; children are e.g. insurance for old age. Marriages move from economic necessity to \&apos;hedonic marriage\&apos; for co-consumption. Children are luxury consumption goods! {Awesome.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Marglin:} the losses outweigh the gains. [how measured?] What economists leave out, he reckons - people are given. But people are shaped by their human relations. Children are not luxury consumption goods. [does anyone actually think this {way?]{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Wants} to argue that he is not romanticising often oppressive aspects of {community.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Wilkinson} asks again for measurable. Marglin: people who are shaped by relationships cannot be measured. He argues that because people are defined by their relationships, people in different social situations \&apos;are not the same person any more\&apos; and so any measurable comparison is meaningless. {Bah.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Wilkinson:} people *are* incredibly socially embedded beings. He argues that neoliberal social structures - \&quot;watching other people consume, watching advertising, watching television\&quot; - are fine. What measure is there for privileging one social structure over {another?{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&quot;That\&apos;s} why I always end up resorting back to the reductive social scientific measures because I want to make sure that people who are fully investing in a liberal market society are or aren\&apos;t in fact worse off on some measureable dimension. Because if all it is, according to some conception of meaning, they\&apos;re worse off, I\&apos;m worried that lots of people have lots of different conceptions of meaning, and we won\&apos;t be able to sort anything out in the long {run.\&quot;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Marglin:} {\&quot;Not} so easy to answer. The attempt to settle these kind of things by some kind of measurable, quantifiable data that is reducible to individuals is already to prejudge the outcome. It\&apos;s already to put it in a framework of economics. When you raise the question of how do you balance one set of obligations as against another - that\&apos;s exactly the sort of question I think we ought to be asking. It\&apos;s not a question that comes out of the economic way of understanding the world or being in the world, because for the economist there\&apos;s no such thing as obligation. There\&apos;s interest but no obligation. You may enter into an obligation to further an interest but the primary thing is your interest. The obligation is a means to an {end.\&quot;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}Notes} that the balance between the individual and the various communities / social networks they\&apos;re part of is a vital question. Hopes the book will lead to more quantitative data, but his book is not about that.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}goes on to various overlaying social connections. Compare this to the book on {USSR} I\&apos;m reading - the overlaying obligations and hierarchies in the factory / party / komsomol... {\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\#\#notes taken up to 36:30{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}\&nbsp;{\textless}/p{\textgreater}}
},

